Build two copies of a serving stack so a patch can be measured in the model.
Patches, each with the PORT.txt beside it naming its repository and base commit:
{% for p in patches %}  {{ p }}
{% endfor %}
- Arm A is every touched repository at its base commit; arm B is the same with the patches.
  Clone fresh for each arm; never modify the repository PORT.txt names.
- Build both the same way, as the repository builds, into venv-A and venv-B here, each
  layered over the framework's venv and holding only what you rebuilt.
- Check that each arm imports its rebuilt package from its own venv and that the framework
  still imports.
{%- if launcher %}
- The run's launcher {{ launcher }} then measures each arm, under FRAMEWORK_PYTHON=<the
  arm's python>. Whatever it established on the build it was written on -- which kernels a
  node runs, laid out per launch -- it must establish again per arm, and work an arm does
  once on first use (a weight prepack, a JIT) stays before what it measures. Under each arm
  run `xe-forge-skill capture run {{ launcher }} -o <arm>-check/raw` and `xe-forge-skill
  capture parse <arm>-check/raw -o <arm>-check/capture.json` until both pass for both arms,
  changing the launcher but never the workload (meta.json's runtime the same for both).
{%- endif %}
- Write arms.json {"A": "<venv-A python>", "B": "<venv-B python>"}, or {"error": "<why>"}
  if an arm cannot be built or measured, and in report.md what you built, how, and what you
  changed in the launcher.
